findings.body:
`resolve_dirs()` now runs at the top of `ios::command`, for every subcommand:

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/ios/mod.rs" lines="103-120"}

`xcode-script` deliberately changes the working directory to the project before resolving dirs. Without npm/pnpm/deno, Xcode runs it from `src-tauri/gen/apple`:

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/ios/xcode_script.rs" lines="70-92"}

`resolve_dirs()` uses `OnceLock::get_or_init`, so the first call wins. The `resolve_dirs()` at line 92 just returns whatever was resolved from `gen/apple`. `resolve_tauri_dir` only looks in the current directory, in `./src-tauri`, and downward (`lookup` with max depth 3). `gen/apple` has no `tauri.conf.json` underneath it, so this panics with "Couldn't recognize the current folder as a Tauri project". The `set_current_dir` workaround now never takes effect.

::callout{type="warning"}
This breaks `cargo tauri ios build` / `ios dev` for anyone using the Cargo-installed CLI (or bun < 1.2), because the Xcode build phase calls `xcode-script`.

Before this PR, each subcommand resolved dirs itself, after any working-directory change. The simplest fix is to stop resolving eagerly in `ios::command`. Resolve inside `Init` and `Build` the way `Dev`, `Run` and `XcodeScript` already do:

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/ios/mod.rs" lines="105-117"}
  match cli.command {
    Commands::Init(options) => init_command(
      MobileTarget::Ios,
      options.ci,
      options.reinstall_deps,
      options.skip_targets_install,
      options.config,
      &crate::helpers::app_paths::resolve_dirs(),
    )?,
    Commands::Dev(options) => dev::command(options, noise_level)?,
    Commands::Build(options) => {
      build::command(options, noise_level, &crate::helpers::app_paths::resolve_dirs()).map(|_| ())?
    }

I'd make the same change in `mobile/android/mod.rs` for consistency, even though `android-studio-script` doesn't change its working directory today.

This refactor PR also changes behavior in the generated Apple metadata:

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/ios/mod.rs" lines="269-276"}

The old code used `None` when no features were set, so cargo-mobile2 didn't render iOS features. Now an empty `Vec` is always passed. That may be harmless if every consumer treats `Some([])` like `None`. But it isn't mentioned in the PR or the changeset (which says "no user facing changes"), and it's unrelated to removing statics. If it's not intentional, restore the original conditional:

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/ios/mod.rs" lines="272-272"}
      features: if ios_options.features.is_empty() {
        None
      } else {
        Some(ios_options.features)
      },

A few small leftovers from the mechanical rewrite:

::diff{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/mod.rs" lines="476-477"}

`tauri_dir` is already a `PathBuf` on line 476, so `.to_path_buf()` on line 477 is an extra clone. Use `tauri_dir.clone()`, or convert only once.

In `xcode_script.rs`, `let cli_options = { read_options(&tauri_config) };` has an unnecessary block (line 99). Line 117 then calls `read_options(&tauri_config)` again, shadowing the `cli_options` value computed just above. That was already true before the PR, but now the outer value is available and could be reused.

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/mobile/ios/xcode_script.rs" lines="97-120"}

Making `ConfigMetadata` an owned value and introducing a `Mutex` only where it's needed (the dev/watch loop) removes a lot of `.lock().unwrap().as_ref().unwrap()` chains and the hidden "config not loaded" failure mode. In `run_dev_watcher`, the guard used in the `if` condition is a temporary and is dropped before the body runs `config.lock()` again, so there's no self-deadlock:

::code{file="crates/tauri-cli/src/interface/rust.rs" lines="595-605"}

Switching `resolve()` to `get_or_init` also removes the `expect("tauri dir already resolved")` panic when a command resolves dirs more than once (e.g. `android run` → `build::run`).
